The syntax error at line 113, column 85 is triggered by the use of the subtraction operator in the quantifier domain "no c2: (Classification - c)" within the requestClassification function. Although the previous fix attempt tried to address this by parenthesizing the subtraction, the core diagnostic issue is that in Alloy 6, when you use a singleton variable (like c: Classification) inside a set subtraction (Classification - c), "c" is a singleton element but the subtraction expects a set. Alloy requires the right-hand side of the subtraction to be a set, not a singleton, so referencing "c" directly may cause type errors. The correct way in Alloy 6 is to write "c: set Classification" or use "c: one Classification" and then subtract "c" as a set, i.e., "Classification - (c)" or "Classification - c", but if "c" is not of type "set Classification", a type error may occur. The real issue is that inside the function's set comprehension, "c" is not in scope as a set but as a single element, so set subtraction may be ambiguous or ill-typed.

