Previous Lessons:
1. Trailing commas after the last field in a signature declaration are not allowed in Alloy 6 and will trigger syntax errors; always ensure the closing brace follows the last field directly.
2. Persistent syntax errors in predicate headers usually result from missing or incorrect type annotations for parameters in Alloy 6. Always check that every parameter in a predicate header is explicitly typed and the delimiter (comma) is used correctly.
3. In Alloy 6, set subtraction in quantifier domains must be parenthesized, i.e., "no c2: (Classification - c) | ..." instead of "no c2: Classification - c | ...".
4. When Alloy reports "There are 3 possible tokens that can appear here: ,  : =", it almost always means the predicate/function parameter list is malformed for the current Alloy version; always double-check exact header formatting and spacing for Alloy 6 compliance before further modeling.
5. [LESSON]: In Alloy 6, always open util/seq before using seq[...] types; forgetting to import the module will cause otherwise valid seq[...] usages to trigger syntax errors.

