[DIAGNOSIS]:
The syntax error at line 113, column 92 is not simply due to a missing parenthesis or set subtraction in a quantifier domain. Instead, it is caused by incorrect bracing: in Alloy, a function body defined with curly braces ({ ... }) is interpreted as a set comprehension, not as a block. The current code uses curly braces for a function intended to return one Classification, but the body is a set comprehension, not an expression returning exactly one value. Alloy 6 expects the function body to be a single expression producing a value of the declared return type, not a set comprehension block. This improper use of curly braces for the function body is the root cause of the syntax error.

[FIX INTENT]:
Replace the set comprehension block in the requestClassification function with an explicit expression that selects the unique Classification value, using the one and some quantifiers to ensure exactly one such value exists.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: Replace the entire body of the requestClassification function (lines 111–114) with a single Alloy expression, rather than a curly-brace set comprehension.
- Step 2: Use the one quantifier to select the unique Classification c in r.affects.classification such that no higher classification exists in r.affects.classification, as follows:

```alloy
fun requestClassification(r: Request): one Classification {
    one c: r.affects.classification | 
        no c2: r.affects.classification | c2 != c and higherClass(c2, c)
}
```
- Step 3: Remove the curly braces surrounding the function body, as Alloy 6 functions expect a single expression (or quantifier expression), not a set comprehension.

[RATIONALE]:
This fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it uses a quantifier expression as the function body (not a set comprehension block), directly producing the required single Classification; it is different from failed attempts, which only adjusted parentheses or quantifier domains inside a set comprehension without addressing the root issue of function body syntax.

[LESSON]:
When defining functions in Alloy 6 that must return a single element, use quantifier expressions (e.g., one c: ...) instead of set comprehension blocks, as curly braces in function bodies will yield set-valued expressions and can trigger syntax errors if not used with the correct context.
