  {  
      one c: r.affects.classification |  
          no c2: r.affects.classification | c2 != c and higherClass[c2, c]  
  }  
  with a single quantifier expression:  
  one c: r.affects.classification | no c2: r.affects.classification | c2 != c and higherClass[c2, c]
- Step 3: Ensure there are no curly braces enclosing the quantifier expression; the function body is only the quantifier expression.

[RATIONALE]:  
This fix is Alloy 6 compliant because function bodies must be single expressions or quantifier expressions (not set comprehensions in curly braces), and this approach directly provides a valid Alloy 6 function definition, which is different from failed approaches that may have tried enclosing the quantifier in braces or otherwise misused comprehension syntax.

[LESSON]:  
